---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Pole Saws",
    "url": "/best-pole-saw/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best String Trimmers",
    "url": "/best-string-trimmer/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Leaf Blowers",
    "url": "/best-leaf-blower/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Weed Eaters",
    "url": "/best-weed-eater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Lawn Edgers",
    "url": "/best-lawn-edger/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Leaf Vacuums",
    "url": "/best-leaf-vacuum/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-10-06", modified: "2026-10-06" });
const title = "8 Best Hedge Trimmers in 2026: Battery, Corded & Gas Compared";
const description =
  "Eight hedge trimmers compared on cut capacity, blade length, weight and power type, from 1-inch battery models to corded, gas and pole trimmers for tall hedges.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "ego-ht2411",
    "name": "EGO Power+ 24-Inch Brushless Hedge Trimmer Kit (HT2411)",
    "category": "24\" Battery, 56V Brushless",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 2000,
    "pros": [
      "Rated for branches up to 1 inch, the class-leading figure for a homeowner cordless trimmer",
      "24-inch dual-action, hardened steel blades running at up to 3,000 strokes per minute",
      "Electric brake stops the blades quickly when you release the trigger",
      "Blades are fully serviceable, so they can be removed and sharpened",
      "Kit includes a 56V 2.5Ah battery and charger, backed by a 5-year limited warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The 56V pack makes it heavier in the hand than 18V and 20V trimmers",
      "No rotating rear handle, so vertical cuts on tall hedges take more wrist work"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The trimmer we would buy for an ordinary suburban hedge line: the 1-inch cut capacity means it rarely stalls, and the EGO battery runs the rest of the yard too.",
    "description": "EGO launched the HT2411 as the first cordless hedge trimmer rated to cut 1-inch branches, and that number is the reason it sits at the top of this list. Most budget battery trimmers are rated at 3/4 inch, which sounds close but is the difference between slicing through last year's privet growth and chewing on it. The 24-inch dual-action blades are hardened steel and move at up to 3,000 strokes per minute, driven by a brushless motor that EGO says is built for longer runtime and lighter weight than a brushed design. Two details show it was designed by people who trim hedges. The electric brake stops the blades quickly when you let go of the trigger, which matters when you are reaching into a shrub near your other hand, and the blades are fully serviceable, so a nicked or dull set can be pulled, sharpened and refitted rather than replaced. The kit ships with a 2.5Ah battery and charger, and the 5-year limited warranty is generous for the category. The honest drawbacks are weight and handle design. A 56V pack is not light, and without a rotating handle you twist your wrist on vertical faces. If you own EGO already, buy the bare HT2410 version and use the battery you have."
  },
  {
    "id": "ego-ht2601",
    "name": "EGO Power+ 26-Inch Hedge Trimmer Kit with Carbon Fiber Rail (HT2601)",
    "category": "26\" Battery, 56V Brushless",
    "badge": "Best for Big, Overgrown Hedges",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.8,
    "reviewCount": 2700,
    "pros": [
      "Up to 1.2-inch cut capacity, the highest of any battery trimmer here",
      "Cuts at up to 3,400 strokes per minute",
      "Carbon fiber rail is covered by a lifetime warranty",
      "Five-position, 180-degree rotating rear handle for vertical cuts",
      "Serviceable gearbox makes blade sharpening simpler"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Costs noticeably more than the HT2411 for gains most small yards will not use",
      "A 26-inch bar is harder to steer around small, rounded shrubs"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The upgrade pick for long, thick hedges and anyone cutting back growth that has been ignored for a season or two.",
    "description": "The HT2601 is what EGO built when it decided the HT2411 was not enough for people with serious hedges. The headline is a cut capacity of up to 1.2 inches, the highest figure on any battery trimmer in this guide, paired with 26-inch dual-action steel blades running at up to 3,400 strokes per minute. Those numbers translate into a tool that keeps moving through thick, woody growth instead of bogging down. Two design changes are as useful as the extra capacity. The rail that carries the blades is carbon fiber, an industry first according to EGO, and the company backs it with a lifetime warranty. And the rear handle rotates through 180 degrees with five positions, so when you turn the trimmer on its side to square up the face of a tall hedge, your wrist stays straight. EGO quotes up to 60 minutes of runtime with the included 2.5Ah battery, and the gearbox is serviceable, which makes sharpening the blades a realistic owner job. The trade-offs are cost and size. A 26-inch bar is excellent on a 60-foot run of arborvitae and less nimble around foundation shrubs and topiary. If your hedges are trimmed every year and rarely get thicker than pencil-width growth, the HT2411 does the job for less money."
  },
  {
    "id": "makita-xhu02z",
    "name": "Makita 18V LXT 22-Inch Hedge Trimmer, Tool Only (XHU02Z)",
    "category": "22\" Battery, 18V LXT",
    "badge": "Best for Makita Owners",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 2400,
    "pros": [
      "22-inch dual-action, double-sided shear blade",
      "Up to 2,700 strokes per minute from a Makita-built motor",
      "Rated at 87 dB(A), noticeably quieter than most cordless trimmers",
      "Up to 1.5 hours of runtime on the low setting with an 18V LXT 4.0Ah battery",
      "Five cushioned anti-vibration elements in the housing"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Sold bare, so it only makes sense if you already own LXT batteries",
      "Not built for branches as thick as the EGO trimmers handle"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If there are already Makita 18V batteries on your shelf, this is a quiet, well-balanced trimmer that costs you nothing in new chargers or packs.",
    "description": "Makita's XHU02Z is the trimmer we point Makita owners to, because the cheapest battery is the one you already have. It uses a 22-inch dual-action, double-sided shear blade, which means you can cut on both the pull and the push stroke and sweep back and forth across a hedge face without flipping the tool. The motor delivers up to 2,700 strokes per minute, and Makita rates runtime at up to an hour and a half on the low setting with a single 18V LXT 4.0Ah battery, enough for most homeowners' entire hedge line in one go. Two things stand out in use. Makita rates it at 87 dB(A), which it says is 25 percent quieter than other cordless hedge trimmers, a real advantage if you trim early on a weekend morning. And the anti-vibration design uses five cushions inside the housing, which takes the buzz out of your hands on longer sessions. Makita lists it at roughly 7.4 pounds with a battery fitted. The limitation is capacity. A 22-inch blade on an 18V tool is built for maintenance trimming of current-season growth, not hacking back neglected hedges with thumb-thick stems. For that, a 56V EGO or a gas trimmer is the better tool. For regular shaping, the Makita is pleasant, quiet and economical."
  },
  {
    "id": "greenworks-ht40b212",
    "name": "Greenworks 40V 24-Inch Cordless Hedge Trimmer Kit (HT40B212)",
    "category": "24\" Battery, 40V",
    "badge": "Best Value With a 1-Inch Cut",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 540,
    "pros": [
      "1-inch cut capacity at a mid-range price",
      "24-inch dual-action steel blades",
      "Rotating rear handle for comfortable vertical trimming",
      "Three-sided wraparound front handle",
      "Kit includes a 2.0Ah USB battery and charger"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Brushed motor, so it is less efficient than the brushless EGO and DeWalt designs",
      "The included 2.0Ah battery limits long sessions without a spare"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheapest way to get a 1-inch cut capacity and a rotating handle in one tool, especially if you already run Greenworks 40V gear.",
    "description": "Greenworks' 24-inch 40V trimmer earns its spot by offering two features that usually cost more: a 1-inch cut capacity and a rotating rear handle. Plenty of trimmers in this price band are rated at 3/4 inch and have a fixed handle, so on paper this is the value pick of the cordless field. The 24-inch steel blades are dual-action, and Greenworks rates the tool at 2,800 strokes per minute. The rear handle rotates so you can cut vertical hedge faces with your wrist in a neutral position, and the three-sided wraparound front handle gives you a secure hold whether the bar is horizontal across the top of a hedge or turned on its side. Greenworks quotes up to 60 minutes of runtime with a fully charged 2.0Ah battery, which is realistic for light trimming but optimistic for thick growth. The bare tool weighs about 5.7 pounds according to Greenworks, before you add the pack. The motor is brushed rather than brushless, which is the main place the price is saved. In practice that means a little less runtime per charge and a tool that runs warmer on heavy work. For a homeowner shaping a few hedges and shrubs every few weeks, it is a sensible buy, and the 40V battery runs a large range of Greenworks yard tools."
  },
  {
    "id": "black-decker-lht2436",
    "name": "BLACK+DECKER 40V MAX 24-Inch Cordless Hedge Trimmer Kit (LHT2436)",
    "category": "24\" Battery, 40V MAX",
    "badge": "Best Budget Cordless",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 7100,
    "pros": [
      "24-inch dual-action blades with a 3/4-inch cut capacity",
      "POWERDRIVE transmission adds torque in thicker growth",
      "Rated for up to 6,000 square feet of hedges per charge",
      "Full-length trigger and wraparound front handle",
      "Battery state-of-charge indicator"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "3/4-inch capacity means it struggles with older, woody stems",
      "The included battery is a small pack, so big jobs need a recharge"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A proven, inexpensive cordless trimmer for tidy hedges that get cut regularly, from a brand with thousands of satisfied owners.",
    "description": "The LHT2436 has been one of the best-selling cordless hedge trimmers for years, and its rating count reflects that. It is not the most powerful trimmer here, but it is honest about what it does. The 24-inch dual-action blades are rated for branches up to 3/4 inch, and BLACK+DECKER's POWERDRIVE transmission is designed to add torque so the blades keep moving through tougher stems; the company claims it cuts twice as many 3/4-inch branches as its older NHT524. BLACK+DECKER rates a charge at up to 6,000 square feet of actual hedge, which is plenty for a typical front yard and side fence line. The ergonomics are thoughtful for a budget tool. A full-length trigger lets you grip the rear handle wherever is comfortable, the wraparound front handle works in both horizontal and vertical cutting positions, and a charge indicator on the pack tells you whether to swap before you start. Where it falls short is capacity. A 3/4-inch rating covers most current-season growth on boxwood, privet and holly, but stems from a hedge that skipped a year will slow it down. The 2-year warranty is shorter than EGO's. If your hedges get trimmed two or three times a season, though, this is all the trimmer you need."
  },
  {
    "id": "black-decker-behts300",
    "name": "BLACK+DECKER 20-Inch Corded Hedge Trimmer with Saw (BEHTS300)",
    "category": "20\" Corded Electric, 3.8 Amp",
    "badge": "Best Corded",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 1500,
    "pros": [
      "3.8-amp motor and 20-inch dual-action hardened steel blade",
      "Cuts branches up to 3/4 inch, and up to 1.5 inches with the built-in SAWBLADE tip",
      "About 5.5 pounds with no battery to carry",
      "Cord retention keeps the extension cord from pulling loose",
      "Unlimited runtime and nothing to charge"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The cord limits range and is easy to cut if you get careless",
      "20-inch blade means more passes on long hedges"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For small yards near an outlet, nothing cheaper cuts as cleanly, and the saw tip handles the odd thick branch a hedge trimmer normally cannot.",
    "description": "If your hedges are within 100 feet of an outdoor outlet, a corded trimmer is the cheapest, lightest way to do the job, and the BEHTS300 is the one we would buy. Its 3.8-amp motor drives a 20-inch dual-action hardened steel blade rated for branches up to 3/4 inch. The clever part is the SAWBLADE at the tip of the bar, which lets you cut branches up to 1.5 inches thick, the stray woody stem that would otherwise mean fetching loppers or a pruning saw. With no battery, the trimmer weighs about 5.5 pounds, so you can hold it at shoulder height for longer than most cordless tools. A cord retention slot keeps the extension cord from pulling out of the plug every time you step forward, which is a small detail that removes the most annoying thing about corded trimmers. The downsides are the ones every corded tool has. The cord limits where you can work, and it is easy to drape the cord over the hedge and cut it, which is why we cover cord handling in the safety section. A 20-inch blade also means more passes on long runs than a 24-inch trimmer. For townhouse yards, small front gardens and anyone who trims a couple of shrubs a month, it is excellent value."
  },
  {
    "id": "echo-hc-2020",
    "name": "ECHO 20-Inch 21.2cc Gas Hedge Trimmer (HC-2020)",
    "category": "20\" Gas, 21.2cc 2-Stroke",
    "badge": "Best Gas",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 150,
    "pros": [
      "21.2cc professional-grade 2-stroke engine",
      "20-inch double-sided, double-reciprocating RazorEdge blades",
      "Tool-free air filter cover and two-stage air filtration",
      "Grease fitting for easy gearbox maintenance",
      "5-year consumer warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About 10 pounds dry, plus fuel, and loud enough to need hearing protection",
      "Needs mixed fuel, seasonal carburetor care and more maintenance than battery tools"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The trimmer for rural properties and long hedgerows where all-day runtime matters more than noise and upkeep.",
    "description": "Battery trimmers have taken over most suburban yards, but a gas trimmer still makes sense in one situation: long hedgerows, far from an outlet, where you want to work for hours on a tank you can refill in a minute. The ECHO HC-2020 is our pick for that job. It runs a 21.2cc professional-grade 2-stroke engine and drives 20-inch double-sided, double-reciprocating blades. ECHO's RazorEdge blades have three sharpened edges on each tooth, and the company says that gives the trimmer twice as many cutting edges as competing models. Maintenance is designed to be done in the field. The air filter cover comes off without tools, the two-stage air filtration extends service intervals, and a grease fitting on the gearbox makes lubrication a two-minute job. ECHO lists it at 10 pounds dry, with a 13.2-ounce transparent fuel tank, and backs it with a 5-year consumer warranty. The costs are real, though. It is heavier and much louder than any battery trimmer here, it needs mixed 2-stroke fuel, and a carburetor left with old fuel over winter will not start in spring. If you trim a few shrubs a month, buy battery. If you are cutting a quarter mile of farm hedge, buy this."
  },
  {
    "id": "ego-hta2000",
    "name": "EGO Power+ 20-Inch Hedge Trimmer Attachment for Multi-Head System (HTA2000)",
    "category": "20\" Pole Hedge Attachment, 56V",
    "badge": "Best Pole Hedge Trimmer",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 1100,
    "pros": [
      "20-inch steel blades with a 1-inch cut capacity",
      "12-position articulating head for cutting tops and faces from the ground",
      "IPX4 water-resistant construction",
      "Fits EGO PH1400 and PH1420 power heads and the EP7500 extension pole",
      "Also sold as the MHT2001 kit with power head, battery and charger"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Needs an EGO power head, so it is expensive if you are starting from nothing",
      "A pole trimmer is slower and more tiring than a handheld one on low hedges"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The safe way to reach the top of a tall hedge without a ladder, and a natural add-on if you already run an EGO power head.",
    "description": "Any hedge taller than about your own height is a reason to stop and think before climbing a stepladder with a running trimmer. A pole hedge trimmer solves that problem, and EGO's HTA2000 is the one we recommend because it plugs into a system many yard owners already have. It is an attachment for EGO's Multi-Head System, compatible with the PH1400 and PH1420 power heads and the EP7500 extension pole, and EGO also sells it as the MHT2001 kit with a power head, 2.5Ah battery and charger. The cutting head uses 20-inch steel blades with a 1-inch cut capacity, the same rating as EGO's flagship handheld trimmer, and it articulates through 12 positions. That articulation is the whole point: angle the head flat to cut across the top of a 9-foot hedge while you stand on the ground, or straighten it to run down the face. The construction is IPX4 water resistant, so damp morning foliage is not a concern. EGO quotes up to 35 minutes of runtime with the kit's 2.5Ah battery, which is enough for tops and high faces when the rest is done with a handheld trimmer. The catch is cost if you do not own a power head, and pole trimming is genuinely slower and more tiring than handheld work on anything below shoulder height."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Hedge Trimmers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-hedge-trimmer/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best hedge trimmer for most homeowners?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a typical suburban hedge, a 22- to 24-inch battery trimmer with a 1-inch cut capacity is the best balance of power, weight and convenience. The EGO HT2411 is our top pick. If you already own Makita, Greenworks or BLACK+DECKER batteries, buy that brand's trimmer instead, because the battery is the expensive part."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a cordless hedge trimmer as good as a gas one?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For homeowners, yes. The best battery trimmers now match small gas trimmers in cutting performance, start instantly, are far quieter and need no fuel or carburetor care. Gas still wins on runtime for long hedgerows and commercial work, because you can refuel in seconds, but most yards will never use that advantage."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What blade length do I need for a hedge trimmer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For small shrubs and rounded shapes, 16 to 20 inches is easier to control. For typical hedges, 22 to 24 inches is the sweet spot. Long, straight hedges benefit from 26 inches. Remember that you need to reach at least halfway across the top from each side, so wide hedges may need a pole trimmer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What does cut capacity mean on a hedge trimmer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Cut capacity is the thickest branch the manufacturer says the blades can cut, set mainly by the gap between the teeth. Budget trimmers are usually rated at 5/8 to 3/4 inch, better battery models at 1 inch, and the EGO HT2601 at up to 1.2 inches. Treat it as a maximum, not an everyday size."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should hedge trimmer blades be sharpened?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most homeowners, once a season is enough, or sooner if the trimmer starts tearing leaves rather than cutting cleanly. Clean and oil the blades after every use, because sap buildup causes much of the poor cutting people blame on dull blades. Use a flat file and follow each tooth's existing bevel."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a hedge trimmer cut branches?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only small ones within its rated cut capacity, typically 3/4 to 1 inch. Some corded trimmers, like the BLACK+DECKER BEHTS300, add a small saw at the tip for branches up to 1.5 inches. Anything thicker should be cut with loppers, a pruning saw or a pole saw to avoid damaging the blades."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a pole hedge trimmer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You need one if your hedge is taller than you can comfortably reach or wider than you can reach halfway across. A pole trimmer lets you cut the top from the ground instead of from a ladder. For hedges below head height, a handheld trimmer is faster and easier to control."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Lawn & Yard Care",
    tags: [
      "best hedge trimmer",
      "best cordless hedge trimmer",
      "battery hedge trimmer",
      "electric hedge trimmer",
      "pole hedge trimmer",
      "gas hedge trimmer"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            8 Trimmers Compared
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Hedge Trimmers</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Cut capacity, not blade length, decides whether a trimmer shears your hedge or chews it. Here is how to match the trimmer to the hedge, then the battery to the rest of your yard.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#ego-ht2411"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Top Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="9.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
