            2. Cut Capacity Matters More Than Blade Length
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most shoppers compare hedge trimmers by blade length, but the number that decides whether a trimmer cuts cleanly or chews and stalls is cut capacity. It is set mostly by the gap between the teeth: a branch has to fit in the gap before the blades can shear it. A trimmer rated at 3/4 inch will slice soft current-season growth all day and then jam on a woody stem from two years ago.
          </p>

          <p>
            For homeowner trimmers, ratings fall into three bands. Budget corded and battery tools usually claim 5/8 to 3/4 inch. Better battery trimmers, including the EGO HT2411, the Greenworks 40V and EGO's pole attachment, claim 1 inch. The EGO HT2601 claims up to 1.2 inches. Treat the figure as a maximum for green wood cut slowly, not a size you should feed it all afternoon.
          </p>

