        <div id="power-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Battery vs Corded vs Gas
          </h2>

          <p>
            Battery trimmers are now the default for good reason. They start instantly, are quiet enough to use without annoying the neighbors, need no fuel or tune-ups, and the best of them cut as well as gas did a decade ago. The real cost is the battery. If you already own a platform, a bare tool is cheap; if you do not, choose the trimmer whose battery runs your mower, blower and <a href="/best-string-trimmer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">string trimmer</a> as well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Corded trimmers are the cheapest and lightest option and never run out of charge. They make sense when every hedge is within reach of an outlet and a 100-foot cord. The limits are range and the cord itself, which drags through flower beds and is easy to cut. Use an outdoor-rated extension cord sized for the length: a light 16-gauge cord is fine for a 3.8-amp trimmer at 100 feet, but thinner cords waste power and run warm.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas trimmers are now a specialist tool. They suit long rural hedgerows, commercial crews and anyone working far from power for hours at a time. You pay for that runtime with weight, noise, mixed fuel and the maintenance every small 2-stroke engine needs. For a suburban lot, we would not buy one.
          </p>

