        <div id="blade-length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Blade Length for Which Hedge
          </h2>

          <p>
            Blade length is about reach and speed, not cutting strength. A longer bar covers more hedge per pass and reaches farther across the top of a wide hedge. A shorter bar is lighter, easier to steer and better for rounded shapes. The mistake is assuming longer is always better: a 26-inch bar is clumsy around a dozen small shrubs.
          </p>

          <p>
            For foundation plantings, boxwood balls and small shrubs, 16 to 20 inches is plenty and easier to control. For the typical suburban hedge, a fence-line privet or a row of holly, 22 to 24 inches is the sweet spot, which is why most of our picks sit there. Long, straight runs of arborvitae, laurel or beech are where a 26-inch bar earns its extra weight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Width matters too. To keep the top of a hedge flat, you need to reach at least halfway across from each side. A 4-foot-wide hedge is comfortable with a 24-inch blade and long arms; a 6-foot-wide hedge usually means a stepladder or a pole trimmer for the middle of the top. Measure before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about the shape you want. Formal hedges with crisp lines reward a longer bar held level, because you can use it almost like a straightedge. Informal, rounded shrubs reward a shorter, lighter tool you can sweep in arcs. Many people with a mix of both are best served by a 22- to 24-inch trimmer and a pair of hand shears for the detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dual-action" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Dual-Action Blades, Speed and Vibration
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every trimmer on our list uses dual-action blades, where both blades move against each other rather than one moving over a fixed blade. Dual action cuts faster and, because the moving masses partly cancel each other, vibrates less. Single-action trimmers still exist at the very bottom of the market, and we would skip them unless the trimmer is for very occasional use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Double-sided blades, with teeth on both edges, let you cut on both the forward and back sweep. That is how professionals work a hedge face, in long, overlapping arcs, and it is noticeably faster than single-sided blades that only cut in one direction. The Makita, ECHO and most of the battery trimmers here are double-sided.
          </p>

          <p>
            Strokes per minute is a useful number, with caveats. Higher speed gives a cleaner cut on soft, leafy growth, because each stem is sheared before it can bend away. But speed under load matters more than free speed, which is why brushless motors and good gearing count. The EGO HT2601 at up to 3,400 strokes per minute and the HT2411 at up to 3,000 sit at the top of this list; most others run around 2,700 to 2,800.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vibration is the hidden cost of a cheap trimmer. Ten minutes of buzzing handles is fine; an hour leaves your hands numb. Makita builds five cushioned elements into the XHU02Z housing for that reason, and both dual-action blades and well-balanced motors help. If you have a lot of hedge to cut, this is worth paying for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Weight, Balance and the Ten-Minute Test
          </h2>

          <p>
            A hedge trimmer is held out in front of your body, often at shoulder height or above, which makes every extra pound feel like three. A tool that feels light in the store can be miserable after ten minutes of holding it level across the top of a hedge. That is why we treat weight and balance as a buying criterion, not an afterthought.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rough ranges are worth knowing. Light corded trimmers like the BEHTS300 weigh about 5.5 pounds. Battery trimmers usually land between 6 and 10 pounds once the pack is fitted, with bigger batteries pushing the total up. Gas trimmers start around 10 pounds dry, before fuel. A pole trimmer feels heavier still, because the weight sits at the end of a long lever.
          </p>

          <p>
            Balance matters as much as total weight. A trimmer that is heavy at the back, where the battery or engine sits, feels lighter when held level than one with the weight forward. Rotating rear handles, like those on the EGO HT2601 and Greenworks 40V, let you keep your wrist straight when the blade is turned vertical, which is where fatigue sets in fastest.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you can, hold the trimmer at arm's length for a minute before you buy. If that is uncomfortable in the store, it will be worse in July. For older users or anyone with shoulder trouble, a lighter tool with a smaller battery is often the better buy, even if it means a recharge halfway through the job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pole-trimmers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. When You Need a Pole Hedge Trimmer
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pole hedge trimmer puts an articulating cutting head on the end of a shaft, so you can reach the top and upper face of a tall hedge from the ground. If your hedge is taller than you are, or wider than you can reach across, one will make the job both faster and safer than working from a stepladder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ladders and hedge trimmers are a bad combination. You are holding a running tool with both hands, so you cannot hold the ladder, and the natural instinct to lean in for one more stretch is exactly how people fall. If you must use a ladder for hedge work, a tripod orchard ladder is far more stable on soft ground than a stepladder; our guide to the <a href="/best-telescoping-ladder/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best telescoping ladders</a> covers what to look for in a ladder that has to stand on uneven ground.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best pole trimmers have heads that articulate through many positions, so you can lay the blade flat across the top or angle it down the face. EGO's HTA2000 attachment offers 12 positions. Many multi-head systems also accept a pole saw head, which is useful if you have overhanging limbs as well as tall hedges; see our guide to the <a href="/best-pole-saw/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best pole saws</a> for how those compare.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pole trimmers are not a replacement for a handheld trimmer. They are slower, more tiring at waist height and harder to control on detail work. Most people with tall hedges are best served by doing the sides with a handheld trimmer and only switching to the pole for the top and the upper third of the faces.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Hedge Trimmer Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            The classic hedge trimmer injury is a cut to the hand that is not on the trigger, usually while clearing a jam or steadying a branch. Every trimmer on our list needs two hands to run, with a switch on the front handle as well as the rear trigger. Never defeat that interlock, and never reach into a hedge to clear a jam with the tool running or the battery in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wear safety glasses at the very least; clippings, dust and sap fly toward your face. Gloves, long sleeves and hearing protection are sensible, and hearing protection is essential with a gas trimmer. Keep children and pets well away, because a trimmer can throw debris several feet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before you start, walk the hedge and look for hidden wire, fencing, string lights and irrigation lines. Check for active bird nests, too: many songbirds nest in hedges in spring and early summer, and in the United States most native species and their active nests are protected by federal law. Light trimming late in the season is the safer habit.
          </p>

          <p>
            With a corded trimmer, run the cord over your shoulder and behind you, so it trails away from the blade, and use the cord retention slot so it cannot pull loose. Never trim near overhead power lines; keep the tool and any pole at least 10 feet away, and call your utility if branches are touching a line. Cutting from a ladder with a running trimmer is the single riskiest habit in hedge work, and it is the reason pole trimmers exist.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Blade Care and Sharpening
          </h2>

          <p>
            A hedge trimmer's blades need more care than most people give them. Sap and resin build up between the blades after every session, which slows the cut, makes the motor work harder and drains batteries faster. Wipe the blades after use, brush out the debris, and spray them with a resin solvent or light lubricating oil before you put the trimmer away. Use the blade cover in storage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Blades dull over time, and a dull trimmer tears leaves instead of shearing them, which leaves brown edges on the hedge a week later. You can touch up most blades with a flat file, following the existing bevel on each tooth and taking the same number of strokes on each. Trimmers with serviceable blades, like the EGO HT2411 and the HT2601's serviceable gearbox, make that easier because the blades can be removed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the blade bolts at the start of every season and after hitting anything hard. A loose blade assembly increases vibration and can let branches jam between the blades. Bent or chipped teeth should be filed smooth; badly damaged blades should be replaced.
          </p>

          <p>
            For gas trimmers, grease the gearbox through the fitting at the interval in the manual, clean the air filter, and either run the tank dry or use stabilized fuel before winter storage. For battery trimmers, store packs indoors at moderate temperatures and partly charged rather than fully charged or flat. Those habits are the difference between a trimmer that lasts three seasons and one that lasts ten. The same routine applies to the rest of your battery yard kit, from your <a href="/best-leaf-blower/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">leaf blower</a> to your <a href="/best-weed-eater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">weed eater</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on Blade Length Instead of Cut Capacity</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A long blade with a 3/4-inch rating will still stall on woody growth. Check the cut capacity first and compare it with the thickest stems in your hedge. If you trim once a year or are taking over a neglected hedge, a 1-inch or higher rating saves you a lot of frustration.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Into a New Battery Platform for One Tool</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bare trimmer is cheap; a battery and charger are not. If you already own batteries from a brand that makes a decent trimmer, buy that one. If you own none, pick the platform that also covers your mower, blower and string trimmer, not just the best trimmer in isolation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trimming From a Stepladder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Holding a running trimmer with both hands means nothing is holding you to the ladder, and leaning for one more reach is how falls happen. Use a pole trimmer for tall hedges, or at least a tripod orchard ladder on firm ground, and never work above shoulder height from a ladder.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forcing Branches Thicker Than the Rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Jamming thick wood into the teeth chips blades, strains the gearbox and leaves a ragged cut. Anything over the trimmer's rated capacity should be cut with loppers or a pruning saw first. Your trimmer will last longer and the hedge will recover faster from clean cuts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting the Trimmer Away Dirty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sap dries between the blades and makes the next session slower, louder and harder on the battery. Brush off debris, wipe the blades, spray them with resin solvent or light oil, and fit the blade cover. It takes two minutes and is the single best thing you can do for blade life.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best hedge trimmer for most homeowners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical suburban hedge, a 22- to 24-inch battery trimmer with a 1-inch cut capacity is the best balance of power, weight and convenience. The EGO HT2411 is our top pick. If you already own Makita, Greenworks or BLACK+DECKER batteries, buy that brand's trimmer instead, because the battery is the expensive part.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a cordless hedge trimmer as good as a gas one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For homeowners, yes. The best battery trimmers now match small gas trimmers in cutting performance, start instantly, are far quieter and need no fuel or carburetor care. Gas still wins on runtime for long hedgerows and commercial work, because you can refuel in seconds, but most yards will never use that advantage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What blade length do I need for a hedge trimmer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For small shrubs and rounded shapes, 16 to 20 inches is easier to control. For typical hedges, 22 to 24 inches is the sweet spot. Long, straight hedges benefit from 26 inches. Remember that you need to reach at least halfway across the top from each side, so wide hedges may need a pole trimmer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does cut capacity mean on a hedge trimmer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Cut capacity is the thickest branch the manufacturer says the blades can cut, set mainly by the gap between the teeth. Budget trimmers are usually rated at 5/8 to 3/4 inch, better battery models at 1 inch, and the EGO HT2601 at up to 1.2 inches. Treat it as a maximum, not an everyday size.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should hedge trimmer blades be sharpened?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners, once a season is enough, or sooner if the trimmer starts tearing leaves rather than cutting cleanly. Clean and oil the blades after every use, because sap buildup causes much of the poor cutting people blame on dull blades. Use a flat file and follow each tooth's existing bevel.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a hedge trimmer cut branches?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only small ones within its rated cut capacity, typically 3/4 to 1 inch. Some corded trimmers, like the BLACK+DECKER BEHTS300, add a small saw at the tip for branches up to 1.5 inches. Anything thicker should be cut with loppers, a pruning saw or a pole saw to avoid damaging the blades.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a pole hedge trimmer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You need one if your hedge is taller than you can comfortably reach or wider than you can reach halfway across. A pole trimmer lets you cut the top from the ground instead of from a ladder. For hedges below head height, a handheld trimmer is faster and easier to control.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

